; ============================================================================
; SET DELETED VISIBILITY -- AIF-123, restoration of a rung lost 2025-08-16
; ============================================================================
;
; WHY THIS SPEC EXISTS, AND WHY IT HAS TO ASSERT BOTH HALVES.
;
; SET DELETED is xBase canon: it decides whether delete-flagged but unpurged
; records are visible to displays and searches. This engine honoured it at
; alpha-v3 -- cmd_list.cpp read Settings::deletedOn() directly. Commit 06ba79e93
; (2025-08-16) rewrote LIST and replaced that read with a per-command flag;
; 523a85e54, the same day, pruned the .sav that still carried the old line. From
; then until 2026-08-24 NOTHING in the tree called Settings::deletedOn().
;
; THE REWRITE PRESERVED THE DEFAULT AND SEVERED THE CONTROL. LIST still hid
; deleted rows, so nobody running LIST saw any change. What broke is invisible
; from the default path: SET DELETED OFF stopped doing anything, because the
; command had stopped asking. Fourteen months, and nothing could go red, because
; the only way to see it is to CHANGE THE SETTING AND NOTICE NOTHING CHANGES.
;
; So this spec asserts BOTH DIRECTIONS, and the OFF half is the discriminator.
;
;   A SPEC THAT ONLY ASSERTS "SET DELETED ON HIDES THE ROW" PASSES GREEN ON A
;   BUILD THAT HIDES DELETED ROWS UNCONDITIONALLY -- which is exactly the build
;   06ba79e93 produced and exactly the build that shipped for fourteen months.
;
; That is the FIELDMGR_APPEND doctrine in a new place: a spec asserting the
; shape of the answer passes on a fixture that cannot give a different one.
; Only contrast proves a switch is connected to anything.
;
; WHAT IS ASSERTED, AND WHY THESE COMMANDS.
;
; GO and SKIP, not LIST and COUNT. Markers here are FIELD-VALUE comparisons per
; FIELDMGR_APPEND, and GO/SKIP always land the cursor on a record whose fields
; can be read. LIST and COUNT report through console text, which no marker can
; read -- their behaviour is visible in the transcript and is NOT claimed by an
; arm. Stated rather than implied.
;
; RECALL is asserted too, and for a different reason -- see half three. It is
; the one verb whose whole purpose is delete-flagged rows, so it is where a
; restoration like this does its damage if the precedence rule is wrong.
;
; NAVIGATION IS TWO FAMILIES, WHICH THIS SPEC'S FIRST RUN DISCOVERED.
;   LOGICAL  -- FIRST / LAST / NEXT / PRIOR, via cli::logical_nav, which calls
;               filter::visible() and therefore honours SET DELETED.
;   PHYSICAL -- GO / SKIP, which move the DbArea cursor directly and consult
;               nothing but SET FILTER.
; Two answers to "where is the next visible record" -- R5, in the navigator.
; The logical family is asserted; the physical family is TRIPWIRED at its
; measured behaviour and named as unfinished.
;
; GO is also the sharpest case in the canon. include/foxpro_go.hpp has said the
; whole time:
;     "If <n> is deleted and SET DELETED ON, FoxPro skips forward to the next
;      undeleted record."
;     "With SET DELETED OFF, GO <n> lands exactly on <n>."
; The specification survived the implementation. These arms are that paragraph.
;
; WHAT A RED ARM DOES AND DOES NOT SEPARATE.
;
; If the SD_ON_* arms red, either the delete flag never landed on record 2 or
; the setting is not consulted. This spec CANNOT tell those apart -- both make
; the row reachable under ON -- and it does not pretend to. Both mean the same
; actionable thing: the delete rung is not working. Distinguishing them is a
; debugging step, and the honest home for "is the flag byte really set" is a
; unit fixture over DbArea::isDeleted(), not a marker.
;
; The SD_OFF_* arms are what make the pair meaningful: they fail on a build that
; hides unconditionally, which no single-direction spec can detect.
;
; Disposable table, rebuilt every run. Leaves nothing behind.
; ============================================================================

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX

; --- fixture: three rows, the middle one delete-flagged ---------------------
SELECT 1
CREATE X64 SDVIS (ID N(8,0), NAME C(20))

APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 1
REPLACE NAME WITH "AliveOne"

APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 2
REPLACE NAME WITH "DeletedRow"

APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 3
REPLACE NAME WITH "AliveTwo"

GO 2
DELETE
CLOSE

; ============================================================================
; HALF ONE -- SET DELETED OFF: the flagged row is VISIBLE
;
; THIS IS THE DISCRIMINATOR. A build that hides deleted rows unconditionally --
; the build this project shipped from 2025-08-16 to 2026-08-24 -- fails every
; arm below while passing every arm in half two.
; ============================================================================

USE SDVIS NOINDEX
SET DELETED OFF

GO 1
? "SD_G0_fixture_built:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "AliveOne")

GO 3
? "SD_G1_third_row_present:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "AliveTwo")

LOCATE FOR ID = 2
? "SD_OFF_T1_locate_reaches_the_flagged_row:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "DeletedRow")

GO 2
? "SD_OFF_T2_go_lands_exactly_on_2:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "DeletedRow")

GO 1
SKIP
? "SD_OFF_T3_skip_steps_onto_the_flagged_row:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "DeletedRow")

; NEXT is the LOGICAL navigator (cmd_next.cpp -> logical_nav -> filter::visible).
; SKIP above is the PHYSICAL one. They are different families and this spec's
; first run proved they answer differently -- see half two.
GO 1
NEXT
? "SD_OFF_T7_next_steps_onto_the_flagged_row:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "DeletedRow")

CLOSE

; ============================================================================
; HALF TWO -- SET DELETED ON: the flagged row is INVISIBLE
;
; This is the half everyone writes. On its own it proves nothing, because it
; also passes on a build with no setting at all that simply always hides.
; ============================================================================

USE SDVIS NOINDEX
SET DELETED ON

GO 1
? "SD_G2_still_the_same_fixture:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "AliveOne")

; --- THE FAMILY THAT IS WIRED ----------------------------------------------
; NEXT goes through cmd_next.cpp -> logical_nav::next_recno -> is_visible ->
; filter::visible(). This is the arm that proves the restored rung actually
; reaches a navigator.
GO 1
NEXT
? "SD_ON_T7_next_steps_over_the_flagged_row:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "AliveTwo")

; --- THE FAMILY THAT IS NOT -- TRIPWIRES, NOT ASPIRATIONS -------------------
;
; SD_ON_T4 AND SD_ON_T5 WERE WRITTEN AS ASSERTIONS AND BOTH READ .F. ON THE
; FIRST RUN, 2026-08-24. They are repointed here to record the measured
; behaviour rather than the intended one -- the IDXSTALE precedent -- because a
; red arm nobody can act on gets muted, and a quietly retuned arm loses the
; finding.
;
; WHAT WAS MEASURED: GO and SKIP do NOT consult SET DELETED. They navigate the
; DbArea directly. Only FIRST / LAST / NEXT / PRIOR go through logical_nav, and
; therefore only those four reach the gate. cmd_skip.cpp:126 consults
; filter::has_active_filter() -- SET FILTER -- and nothing else.
;
; THE AUTHOR'S ERROR, RECORDED: the fix's plan said "logical_nav is already the
; right home, so wiring the gate gets GO/SKIP for free." That was read off
; first/next/prev/last_recno living in logical_nav.cpp and generalised to all
; navigation. GO and SKIP are not in that file and never were.
;
; THIS IS UNFINISHED WORK, NOT ACCEPTED BEHAVIOUR. include/foxpro_go.hpp states
; the canon plainly and has the whole time: "If <n> is deleted and SET DELETED
; ON, FoxPro skips forward to the next undeleted record." Wiring GO and SKIP is
; a further change and wants its own ruling, because it alters what a bare GO
; does. Until then these two lines say what the engine does, out loud.
;
; IF EITHER EVER READS .T., GO/SKIP HAVE GAINED DELETE-FILTERING. Repoint them
; deliberately back to assertions; do not let them retune themselves.
GO 2
? "SD_ON_TRIP1_go_2_still_lands_on_the_flagged_row:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "DeletedRow")

GO 1
SKIP
? "SD_ON_TRIP2_skip_still_steps_onto_the_flagged_row:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "DeletedRow")

CLOSE

; ============================================================================
; HALF THREE -- THE PRECEDENCE RULE: AN EXPLICIT CLAUSE BEATS THE SETTING
;
; RECALL EXISTS TO OPERATE ON DELETE-FLAGGED ROWS. If the session default were
; allowed to reach it, SET DELETED ON would hide every row RECALL is for, and
; RECALL would silently find nothing -- a verb that reports success over an
; empty selection. That is the regression this restoration could most easily
; have shipped, and it was caught by the owner asking about cmd_delete.cpp
; before the code was committed, not by a test.
;
; It does not happen, and the reason is structural rather than lucky: all four
; of cmd_recall's selection paths set deleted_mode = OnlyDeleted, which the gate
; reads as CallerHandles and stays out of. This arm locks that in.
;
; RECALL ALL under SET DELETED ON must clear the flag on record 2. Then GO 2 --
; still under ON -- must land on it, because it is no longer flagged. One arm
; proving both that RECALL reached a row the gate would have hidden, and that
; the gate stops hiding it once it is live.
;
; NOTE, stated because it is a real consequence and not a defect: under SET
; DELETED ON you cannot NAVIGATE onto a flagged row to recall it one at a time --
; GO skips forward and LOCATE does not reach it. That is xBase canon (SET
; DELETED OFF is how you get at a deleted record), and the scoped RECALL forms
; work regardless because they name the clause.
; ============================================================================

USE SDVIS NOINDEX
SET DELETED ON

RECALL ALL

GO 2
? "SD_ON_T6_recall_reached_a_row_the_gate_hides:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "DeletedRow")

CLOSE

; ============================================================================
; RESTORE THE SESSION DEFAULT.
; SET DELETED defaults to ON (settings.hpp: deleted_on{true}). A spec that
; leaves a session setting flipped is a spec that changes the next spec's
; answers, which is how a suite starts depending on its own running order.
; ============================================================================
SET DELETED ON
